<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Deutsche Welle: DW.com - Germany</title><subtitle type="html">Deutsche Welle: DW.com - Germany</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-ger</id><updated>2026-10-21T05:02:45Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-ger" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-1432?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-ger" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Wagner brothers begin new NBA chapter on different paths</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c1c4a17104858ee9be801111eec1c5da027abd9daf4fc2dfb72f9d6edb16e75a</id><updated>2026-10-21T05:01:38Z</updated><published>2026-10-20T12:31:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/wagner-brothers-begin-new-nba-chapter-on-different-paths/a-79712668--ff5544415c4f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/wagner-brothers-begin-new-nba-chapter-on-different-paths/a-79712668?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">After five years, brothers Franz and Moritz Wagner are no longer playing together for the Orlando Magic. In the new NBA season, their separation will likely mean the new "German" team is the Charlotte Hornets.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>AfD's Alice Weidel to unveil plan to cut EU powers — report</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1c9f6d2cca5e19801af62b6c9e7e8cdbcc49fb1a7e351a2a7f9f342f19e4216a</id><updated>2026-10-21T05:02:45Z</updated><published>2026-10-20T22:25:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/afd-s-alice-weidel-to-unveil-plan-to-cut-eu-powers-report/a-79717944--20fa5b0134f0.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/afd-s-alice-weidel-to-unveil-plan-to-cut-eu-powers-report/a-79717944?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The far-right AfD will propose slashing powers of the European Union when it presents its policy paper on Wednesday, Politico reports.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Germany news: Hackers release AfD party emails</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fa89981570b128f74b088d93151c52c7b2819c5d0bd2512d3b07ea1f2a6125ee</id><updated>2026-10-21T05:01:38Z</updated><published>2026-10-20T15:41:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-news-hackers-release-afd-party-emails/live-79709090--0adf9ddcd090.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-news-hackers-release-afd-party-emails/live-79709090?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Germany's far-right AfD has launched an inquiry after the release of thousands of emails following a cyberattack. Also, investors pledged billions for Germany at a major summit in Berlin. DW has more news from Germany.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Germany: Google must have consent to publish property images</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c8cd15551a0962be527aef92d89acd2886df5eec60c9451fcbf59dc106c6c010</id><updated>2026-10-21T05:01:38Z</updated><published>2026-10-20T11:46:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-google-must-have-consent-to-publish-property-images/a-79712552--7cfbdb603d27.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-google-must-have-consent-to-publish-property-images/a-79712552?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">A court in the western German state of Hesse has said Google is "spying" on people and has no right to publish information about their property on its Maps and Earth sites. The case stems from images taken from the air.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Bulgaria seeks suspects in Germany after Starlink sabotage</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:7c9952c379e5e7e785525550251ed813b4c15d51abc2ac1f498e12da5da41420</id><updated>2026-10-21T05:01:38Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T18:45:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/bulgaria-seeks-suspects-in-germany-after-starlink-sabotage/a-79706745--e11bc2352fd6.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/bulgaria-seeks-suspects-in-germany-after-starlink-sabotage/a-79706745?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">A Bulgarian court has issued arrest warrants for two young German nationals suspected of setting fire to Starlink antennas. The case is reminiscent of another in Poland, which Warsaw says was orchestrated by Russia.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Germany news: Emergency at Leipzig airport sparked by novice driver</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f05e5a85ce669159d5a72245bf8e1094685276fcbc5d733f5667e30932649b23</id><updated>2026-10-21T05:01:38Z</updated><published>2026-10-19T17:26:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-news-emergency-at-leipzig-airport-sparked-by-novice-driver/live-79695354--1d0844a30c8b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-news-emergency-at-leipzig-airport-sparked-by-novice-driver/live-79695354?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Two men breached a fence at the Leipzig/Halle airport while practicing for a driving test. Meanwhile, German Chancellor Friedrich Merz announced an industrial AI partnership with Nvidia. DW has more.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Why US investors are snapping up Germany's famed Mittelstand</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:35f64d41bd4688ab096452ca36a85e9fc8ea30b53d5cf0d5fe2329211190cc57</id><updated>2026-10-21T05:01:38Z</updated><published>2026-10-20T09:06:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/why-us-investors-are-snapping-up-germany-s-famed-mittelstand/a-79705421--453454d6f76c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/why-us-investors-are-snapping-up-germany-s-famed-mittelstand/a-79705421?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Increasing amounts of US capital are flowing into Germany's traditional industrial firms and family-owned businesses. As many business owners struggle to find successors, more companies are becoming acquisition targets.</summary><category term="Business" /></entry><entry><title>Is Germany's eight-hour workday under threat?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d1449fc1d80b906d2beb82651ce98fc279263db2dfad8a849bcdb7bf60762f5c</id><updated>2026-10-21T05:01:38Z</updated><published>2026-10-18T10:28:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/is-germany-s-eight-hour-workday-under-threat/a-79680984--28b0ad264d5e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/is-germany-s-eight-hour-workday-under-threat/a-79680984?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Would you work 12 hours straight? The CDU/CSU and SPD are locked in a dispute over reforms to the Working Hours Act. Workplace flexibility, collective bargaining agreements and worker protection rules are at stake.</summary><category term="Germany" /></entry><entry><title>'Ragebait' is the 2026 German Youth Word of the Year</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:224f082c59f6fe29f2c649ce513f9dada024dbce3912240b212e6fcb1bca78ac</id><updated>2026-10-21T05:01:38Z</updated><published>2026-10-10T13:36:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/ragebait-is-the-2026-german-youth-word-of-the-year/a-79608158--c39c4c350c71.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/ragebait-is-the-2026-german-youth-word-of-the-year/a-79608158?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Already named the 2025 Oxford Word of the Year, "rage bait" has also been widely adopted in German slang.</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry><entry><title>Author or artificial intelligence? Literature in crisis</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a2982cfff49673da22c8690a6a13a8e5e149cbe8c5135dcf1fab0e65936f303c</id><updated>2026-10-21T05:01:38Z</updated><published>2026-10-09T15:04:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/author-or-artificial-intelligence-literature-in-crisis/a-79618610--2ddbca94bc5e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/author-or-artificial-intelligence-literature-in-crisis/a-79618610?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">AI is a hot topic at this year's Frankfurt Book Fair. An international literary scandal has raised questions about machine-generated texts.</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry></feed>
